  "prompt": "[system]\n\n    You generate compact, robust Python TSP metaheuristics.\n    You must follow the requested interface exactly, always preserve tour validity.\n    \n\n[user]\nPARENTS:\nCandidate cand_000002: deterministic_2opt\n\nStatus: valid; distance: 8.25341\n\nThought: Start with a nearest neighbor tour from node 0. Then repeatedly apply first-improvement 2-opt: scan edge pairs (i,i+1) and (j,j+1) with i < j-1, if swapping to (i,j) and (i+1,j+1) decreases total distance, reverse the segment [i+1..j] and restart scanning. Continue until no improving move found. Report each new best tour.\n\nHistory source: yes\nHistory bucket: tsp:size:100\nHistory role: regime specialist\n\nCode:\n\n```python\n\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef solve_tsp(distance_matrix: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    n = distance_matrix.shape[0]\n    # Nearest neighbor initial tour\n    tour = [0]\n    unvisited = set(range(1, n))\n    current = 0\n    while unvisited:\n        next_city = min(unvisited, key=lambda x: distance_matrix[current, x])\n        tour.append(next_city)\n        unvisited.remove(next_city)\n        current = next_city\n    tour = np.array(tour, dtype=int)\n    # report initial tour\n    report_best_tour(tour.copy())\n    \n    improved = True\n    while improved:\n        improved = False\n        for i in range(n - 2):\n            for j in range(i + 2, n):\n                # edges (i,i+1) and (j,(j+1)%n)\n                a, b = tour[i], tour[(i+1) % n]\n                c, d = tour[j], tour[(j+1) % n]\n                delta = (distance_matrix[a, c] + distance_matrix[b, d]\n                         - distance_matrix[a, b] - distance_matrix[c, d])\n                if delta < -1e-12:  # improvement\n                    # reverse segment i+1..j\n                    tour[i+1:j+1] = np.flip(tour[i+1:j+1])\n                    improved = True\n                    report_best_tour(tour.copy())\n                    break\n            if improved:\n                break\n    return tour\n\n```\n\nVERBAL GRADIENT: follow the reflection below as the primary source of change.\n\nUse the supplied parents as supporting evidence only.\n\nPARENT-SPECIFIC LLM REFLECTIONS:\n\nParent cand_000002 LLM reflection:\n- Summary: Nearest neighbor plus 2-opt first-improvement yields valid tours with no timeouts and moderate tour length, but may be stuck in local optima.\n- Aim: Improve tour length by incorporating diversification or a better construction heuristic, while maintaining fast runtime and zero timeout rate.\n- Preserve: Nearest neighbor initialization; first-improvement 2-opt; report_best_tour; fast runtime and no timeouts\n- Change: Replace nearest neighbor with a stochastic or multi-start construction heuristic; Add a perturbation step after 2-opt convergence to escape local optima\n- Avoid: Switching to best-improvement 2-opt which may increase runtime and risk timeout; Overcomplicating the algorithm to the point of breaking validity or causing timeouts\n\nParent cand_000005 LLM reflection:\n- Summary: Candidate improved tour length over parent by adding Or-opt to VND, but runtime increased from 0.11s to 1.58s while staying within timeout.\n- Aim: Reduce runtime without sacrificing tour quality, possibly by limiting Or-opt or using more efficient neighborhood exploration.\n- Preserve: Farthest insertion construction; Report_best_tour usage during construction and each improvement; Validity guarantee of permutation\n- Change: Replace full Or-opt with a partial or stochastic variant to reduce runtime; Add an iteration limit or early termination condition for the VND loop\n- Avoid: Increasing runtime beyond timeout threshold for larger instances; Removing report_best_tour calls or breaking validity\n\nDistance is the search objective for TSP; lower distance is better.\n\nImplement a complete TSP metaheuristic inside this required entrypoint:\n\ndef solve_tsp(distance_matrix: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n\nRequirements:\n- Instances are symmetric Euclidean 2D-style TSPs represented by a distance matrix.\n- Minimize total TSP tour distance.\n- Return a 1D np.ndarray containing each node exactly once.\n- Do not repeat the start node at the end.\n- Always return a valid tour.\n- Use only distance_matrix; coordinates are not provided, but metric/geometric structure can be inferred from distances.\n- Create a valid tour early.\n- Call report_best_tour(tour) whenever a new best valid tour is found, assume this func already exists.\n- Do not use files, network, subprocesses, or external solvers.\n- Keep the code compact and robust.\n\ncorrect signature, valid tour on every return path,\nearly report_best_tour, allowed imports only, no I/O/network/subprocesses.\n\nReturn one JSON object and nothing else:\n\n{\n  \"name\": \"short_snake_case_name\",\n  \"thought\": \"high-level summary of the construction, improvement, and tie-break logic\",\n  \"code\": \"complete Python code as a JSON string\"\n}\nNo Markdown, fences, or text outside JSON. The code string must define solve_tsp.",
